course: dictionary
lesson: 15784
title: Bits of words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Ull» is not a word by itself. When you see it, it is usually part of a longer word. Look at the words around it to work out what was meant.
explain: Sometimes it is the end of a word, such as «full», «dull», «skull» or «hull». Other times it is a short form, such as «you'll» or «it'll», where the rest of the word has been squashed up.
explain: Hull, with a capital H, is a city in England. In some accents people drop the h, so you might see it written as «'Ull».
fact: «Ull» on its own is not a standard English word.
fact: Many words end in «-ull», such as «full», «dull», «skull» and «hull».
fact: «You'll» and «it'll» are short forms, where «'ll» stands for «will».
fact: Hull is a city in England, and some people say it as «'Ull».
fact: To work out a cut-off word, read the words around it and ask what would fit.
exercise: «I'm sure you'll love the park.» What does the 'll in you'll stand for? | are | was | have | *will
exercise: «The bowl was full to the top.» Which word ends in -ull like full? | river | happy | *skull | dog
exercise: Ella reads «'Ull is a big city in England.» What is the city called? | Gull | Hall | Dull | *Hull
test: «We'll win the match if we train hard.» What does 'll stand for here? | have | *will | are | was
test: Which word ends in the same letters as «dull»? | apple | river | cloud | *skull
exam: «It'll be fine by Monday.» What is the full form of «it'll»? | *it will | it has | it was | it is
